From: ecjbosu at aol.com (Joe W. Byers)
Date: Fri, 01 Feb 2008 18:20:42 +0000
Subject: rjava problem loading resource
Message-ID: <loom.20080201T173232-99@post.gmane.org>

I have a custom built connector for an oracle Db that we use at my facility.  I
add all these jars to my classpath using
.jaddClassPath(c('/jarpath/jar1.jar','jarpath/jar2.jar'))

I execute a method in jar1.jar
acc<-.jcall('com/company/division/Class', "Z",
  method='getAccess', as.character(ID))

this method will return T or F depending on the connection success or failure. 
this method will implement a connection class that utilizes a xml resource file,
resource1.xml, that is inside jar1.jar.  an error cannot find
pathtoresource/resource1.xml.

My IT people tell it is the way rJava loads the class definitions and I am
curious if this is true?  And how I can get rJava to see the xml file.  I have
tried unarchiving the jar files and using their repective paths.  I still have
the same problem.

Any help is greatly appreciated.

thank you
Joe

From simon.urbanek at r-project.org  Wed Feb  6 17:39:13 2008
From: simon.urbanek at r-project.org (Simon Urbanek)
Date: Wed, 06 Feb 2008 12:39:13 -0500
Subject: Re: rjava problem posted on rosada
In-Reply-To: <07E814A97A1E4C4F9F1FC152F50BC50B016C560C@HOUEXCHMBCS01.bsna.bsroot.bear.com>
References: <07E814A97A1E4C4F9F1FC152F50BC50B016C560C@HOUEXCHMBCS01.bsna.bsroot.bear.com>
Message-ID: <6F59AC5F-400A-43F5-8CCB-95FCB9D1CA1B@r-project.org>

Joe,

yes, rJava uses a custom class loader in order to support custom  
search paths for class files and libraries. However, it's passing a  
request to load resources down to the boot loader and also it looks up  
resources in the JAR files on the class path as well as directories on  
the class path. If you set verbose to anything >0 (e.g. via  
RJavaClassLoader.setDebug(1)), you'll get some messages that may help  
you identify the problem. If I am to help you, you'll have to be more  
specific what code exactly you use to locate the resource and what is  
exactly your layout.

Cheers,
Simon


