From nspmma at freenet.de  Wed Feb 27 03:52:46 2008
From: nspmma at freenet.de (Alberto)
Date: Tue, 26 Feb 2008 19:52:46 -0800
Subject: Images in Mondrian tool tips
Message-ID: <47C4DE8E.7010704@freenet.de>

Hi,
I would like to thank you for making Mondrian available. It is a great tool!

I downloaded the code and implemented a prototype to allow for images to 
be shown in the tool tips of Mondrian.

Since I am a chemist by training the things I look at most frequently 
are data on chemical compounds and as such the chemical structure is an 
important information which is difficult to represent in a tab separated 
file and even more difficult to recognize in a text of the tool tips. I 
was therefore looking for a possibility to include pictures in the tool 
tips Mondrian displays when holding down the shift-ctrl key and moving 
the mouse over data points. It is nice to know that compound 4523341 is 
an outlier, but it is much better to see the chemical structure for that 
outlier. Please find attached two images of the prototype I implemented 
(MondiranToolTip*.jpg).

The basic idea is that the tab separated input file contains some 
identifier which allows Mondrian to fetch the image from a web server. I 
have implemented two examples in the prototype:
Images for protein database entries eg: 
http://www.rcsb.org/pdb/images/1ccz_bio_r_80.jpg?getBest=true
and chemical structures for compounds from the NCBI pubchem database:
http://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/image/imgsrv.fcgi?cid=384259

The basic idea is that there is a hashmap Util.LABELToURLTemplate which 
maps field labels to templates for the html code to be displayed for the 
field. eg. the template for a field labeled "PDB Code" could be:
"<image 
src='http://www.rcsb.org/pdb/images/$val_bio_r_80.jpg?getBest=true'/>"
Whenever a pdb-code is displayed "$val" is replaced by the field value 
(eg. 1ccz) and this is inserted in the tool tip instead of the raw field 
value.

Right now this works only in 2D scatter plots.


I have also attached the java files with the modifications I made:
Util.java
    - added HashMap LABELToURLTemplate with static initialized with hard 
coded mappings for
       "PDB Code" and "NCBI CID"
    - added method getHTMLValue(String label, String val) which uses the 
HashMap to convert the
        raw field to the specific html code.
       To make this work I had to change the separator currently used in 
Mondrian between
        field labels and values from ":" to  "\t"
    - modified info2Html to use "\t" instead of ":" as separator
Scatter2D.java
    - modified getToolTipText to use Util.getHTMLValue and use "\t" 
instead of ":" as separator.
Mosaic.java
    - modified maintainSelection to use "\t" instead of ":" as separator
PC.java
    - modified getToolTipText to use "\t" instead of ":" as separator
MyRect.java
    - modified pop to use "\t" instead of ":" as separator

Join.java
    - to make the fetching of gif files from the class file location 
work with eclipse I modified readGif
       to use "this.getClass().getResourceAsStream". This should be 
independent from the class path
       and work for all environments.


Here are the most serious problems I see with the current prototype:
- Mapping from label to html-template is hard coded in Util.java.
    This needs a configuration file and/or a user interface to edit.

- Works only in Scatter2D. It was not immediately clear to me how I can 
have access to the field label
    in the other classes so I would need some suggestions on how to make 
this work in the other
    visualizations

- Does not work with numerical columns. the reason is that numerical 
columns are stored in
    double fields and by the time they are accessed in the tool tip code 
something that was a 1234
    might very well become a 1234.0. Which obviously might not be a 
valid ID for the image server.
     I would need some input here on how to approach this problem.

I have attached two example input files with data from the pdb database 
and from the NIH web site (pdb.txt and NCBI_AID.txt). which can be used 
together with the modified code to see how nice the prototype already 
works. I have also attached a full jar file so that you can give the 
code a try without recompiling.

Would people be interested in fully integrating this into Mondrian? 
Please let me know what you think!

Thanks,
Alberto

